{"subjectId":"python","id":"type-hints","title":"Type Hints & mypy","group":"Advanced Python","order":43,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Type hints describe what a function expects and returns. Python ignores them at runtime; static checkers (mypy, pyright) and IDEs use them to catch bugs before the code runs.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Gradual typing</strong> — you can add hints a little at a time. Unannotated code is treated as <code>Any</code> (anything goes), so a codebase can move from untyped to fully typed one module at a time.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Annotation basics</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def greet(name: str, times: int = 1) -&gt; str:\n    return \", \".join([f\"hi {name}\"] * times)\n\ncount: int = 0\nscores: dict[str, list[float]] = {\"asha\": [9.5, 8.0]}\npoint: tuple[int, int] = (3, 4)\nids: tuple[int, ...] = (1, 2, 3)      # variable-length tuple\n\nprint(greet(\"ravi\", 2))               # hi ravi, hi ravi\nprint(greet.__annotations__)          # {'name': &lt;class 'str'&gt;, 'times': &lt;class 'int'&gt;, 'return': &lt;class 'str'&gt;}\nprint(greet(42))                      # hi 42   ← runs fine: hints are NOT enforced at runtime</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-details\"></i>The vocabulary</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Hint</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>list[int]</code>, <code>dict[str, int]</code>, <code>set[str]</code></td><td>Built-in generics (3.9+) — no <code>typing.List</code> needed</td></tr><tr><td><code>int | None</code> / <code>Optional[int]</code></td><td>May be <code>None</code> (the <code>|</code> form is 3.10+)</td></tr><tr><td><code>int | str</code> / <code>Union[int, str]</code></td><td>One of several types</td></tr><tr><td><code>Any</code></td><td>Opt out of checking for this value</td></tr><tr><td><code>object</code></td><td>Any value, but you may only use what every object supports — safer than <code>Any</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Callable[[int, str], bool]</code></td><td>A function taking <code>(int, str)</code> and returning <code>bool</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Iterable[T]</code>, <code>Sequence[T]</code>, <code>Mapping[K, V]</code></td><td>Accept the <em>abstract</em> type (from <code>collections.abc</code>) in parameters</td></tr><tr><td><code>Literal[\"r\", \"w\"]</code></td><td>Only these exact values</td></tr><tr><td><code>Final</code></td><td>Must not be reassigned</td></tr><tr><td><code>ClassVar[int]</code></td><td>A class-level attribute, not an instance field</td></tr><tr><td><code>Self</code></td><td>The type of the current class — for fluent methods (3.11+)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Never</code> / <code>NoReturn</code></td><td>Function never returns normally (always raises)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections.abc import Iterable, Callable\nfrom typing import Literal\n\ndef total(xs: Iterable[float]) -&gt; float:              # accepts list, tuple, generator…\n    return sum(xs)\n\ndef apply(f: Callable[[int], int], x: int) -&gt; int:\n    return f(x)\n\ndef open_mode(mode: Literal[\"r\", \"w\"]) -&gt; str:\n    return f\"mode={mode}\"\n\nprint(total([1.5, 2.5]), total(x / 2 for x in range(3)))   # 4.0 1.5\nprint(apply(lambda n: n * n, 7), open_mode(\"w\"))           # 49 mode=w</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Be liberal in what you accept, specific in what you return: take <code>Iterable[str]</code> or <code>Mapping[str, int]</code> as parameters, return concrete <code>list[str]</code> / <code>dict[str, int]</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-braces\"></i>Structured data: TypedDict, NamedTuple, Protocol</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from typing import TypedDict, NotRequired, NamedTuple, Protocol\n\nclass Movie(TypedDict):\n    title: str\n    year: int\n    rating: NotRequired[float]      # key may be absent\n\nclass Point(NamedTuple):\n    x: float\n    y: float\n\nclass SupportsClose(Protocol):      # structural: anything with close() matches\n    def close(self) -&gt; None: ...\n\ndef shutdown(things: list[SupportsClose]) -&gt; int:\n    for t in things:\n        t.close()\n    return len(things)\n\nclass Socket:\n    def close(self) -&gt; None:\n        pass\n\nm: Movie = {\"title\": \"Dune\", \"year\": 2021}\nprint(m[\"title\"], Point(1, 2).x, shutdown([Socket(), Socket()]))   # Dune 1 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets-angle\"></i>Generics: TypeVar and the 3.12 syntax</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from typing import TypeVar\n\nT = TypeVar(\"T\")                          # pre-3.12 style\ndef first_old(xs: list[T]) -&gt; T:\n    return xs[0]\n\ndef first[T](xs: list[T]) -&gt; T:           # 3.12+ style: same meaning\n    return xs[0]\n\ndef largest[N: (int, float)](a: N, b: N) -&gt; N:   # constrained type parameter\n    return a if a &gt; b else b\n\nclass Box[T]:\n    def __init__(self, item: T) -&gt; None:\n        self.item = item\n    def get(self) -&gt; T:\n        return self.item\n\ntype Matrix = list[list[float]]           # type alias statement (3.12+)\n\nprint(first_old([\"a\"]), first([1, 2]), largest(2.5, 1.0), Box(\"x\").get())   # a 1 2.5 x\nprint(Matrix.__value__)                    # list[list[float]]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>Overloads, <code>Self</code>, <code>cast</code> and <code>TYPE_CHECKING</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from typing import overload, Self, cast, TYPE_CHECKING\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:                   # only True for the type checker — avoids import cycles\n    from decimal import Decimal\n\n@overload\ndef parse(x: str) -&gt; int: ...\n@overload\ndef parse(x: bytes) -&gt; str: ...\ndef parse(x):                       # the one real implementation\n    return int(x) if isinstance(x, str) else x.decode()\n\nclass Query:\n    def __init__(self) -&gt; None:\n        self.parts: list[str] = []\n    def where(self, cond: str) -&gt; Self:   # subclasses get their own type back\n        self.parts.append(cond)\n        return self\n\ndef to_money(x: object) -&gt; \"Decimal\":\n    return cast(\"Decimal\", x)       # tells the checker \"trust me\" — no runtime effect\n\nprint(parse(\"42\"), parse(b\"hi\"), Query().where(\"a\").where(\"b\").parts)   # 42 hi ['a', 'b']</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield-check\"></i>Running a type checker</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Terminal</figcaption><pre><code>$ pip install mypy\n$ mypy app.py                     # or: pyright app.py\n$ mypy --strict src/              # require annotations everywhere</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># app.py\ndef average(xs: list[float]) -&gt; float:\n    return sum(xs) / len(xs)\n\naverage([\"1\", \"2\"])            # a bug mypy finds without running anything</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>mypy output</figcaption><pre><code>app.py:5: error: List item 0 has incompatible type \"str\"; expected \"float\"  [list-item]\napp.py:5: error: List item 1 has incompatible type \"str\"; expected \"float\"  [list-item]\nFound 2 errors in 1 file (checked 1 source file)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>Annotations at runtime</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import typing\n\ndef make() -&gt; Widget:          # Widget doesn't exist yet\n    return Widget()\n\nclass Widget:\n    size: int = 3\n\nprint(typing.get_type_hints(make))            # {'return': &lt;class '__main__.Widget'&gt;}\nprint(typing.get_type_hints(Widget))          # {'size': &lt;class 'int'&gt;}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Version</div><p>Since <strong>3.14</strong> annotations are evaluated lazily (PEP 649), so forward references like <code>-&gt; Widget</code> work without quotes or <code>from __future__ import annotations</code>. On 3.13 and older, write <code>-&gt; \"Widget\"</code> or add the <code>__future__</code> import.</p></div>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield\"></i>Runtime validation</h3><p>Hints aren't enforced. Libraries like <strong>pydantic</strong> and <strong>beartype</strong> read them to validate data at runtime.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-code\"></i>Stubs</h3><p><code>.pyi</code> files give types to untyped libraries; many live in <code>types-*</code> packages (<code>pip install types-requests</code>).</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-package\"></i><code>py.typed</code></h3><p>An empty marker file that tells checkers your package ships inline types.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stairs-up\"></i>Gradual rollout</h3><p>Start with public function signatures, run mypy in CI, then tighten with <code>--strict</code> module by module.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>list[int]</code> as a parameter type is <em>invariant</em>: a <code>list[bool]</code> is not accepted even though <code>bool</code> is a subclass of <code>int</code>, because the function could append a plain <code>int</code>. Accept <code>Sequence[int]</code> (read-only, covariant) instead.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Does Python enforce type hints?</summary><div><p>No. The interpreter stores them in <code>__annotations__</code> and otherwise ignores them. Static checkers (mypy, pyright), IDEs and runtime libraries such as pydantic are what give them teeth.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Optional[int] vs a default of None?</summary><div><p><code>Optional[int]</code> (or <code>int | None</code>) says the <em>value</em> may be <code>None</code>. It does not make the parameter optional — that's what a default value does. <code>def f(x: int | None)</code> still requires an argument.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is a Protocol and how does it differ from an ABC?</summary><div><p>A <code>Protocol</code> describes required methods and is matched <strong>structurally</strong> — any class with those methods qualifies, without inheriting. An ABC is <strong>nominal</strong>: a class must inherit from it (or be registered). Protocols suit duck typing; ABCs suit shared implementation and runtime <code>isinstance</code> checks.</p></div></details></section>"}